# authz-step-names-own-credential.tflw — CHECK-ONLY FIXTURE (M130c): an authorization assertion on
# an `api` step that carries its own credential — demonstrates `tflw check`'s TF062 (checker, tflw
# M130b2 / D328, the pentest arc's Tier 2).
#
# This is **not** a style objection to writing headers by hand. The probe strips the observed
# request's `Authorization`/`Cookie` and applies the probing principal's own; the verdict is then a
# comparison between what the *owner's* `as <session>` contributed and what each other principal
# gets. A credential written onto the step belongs to neither side of that comparison — it is an
# identity the run cannot name, held by no session, subtractable from no probe set. A finding
# derived from it is confidently wrong, and it can be wrong in **either** direction: the step's
# hand-written token may be more privileged than the session (a real leak reported as clean, because
# every principal legitimately sees it) or less (a clean route reported as a violation).
#
# **The repair is to move the credential, not to delete it.** Put it in a `session` block and name
# it with `as <session>` — then it is an identity the oracle can both attribute and exclude.
#
# **Only the literal spelling is caught, and the bound is deliberate.** The check reads header
# *names* off the step: `Authorization` and `Cookie`, case-insensitively, and only when the name has
# no `{interpolation}` in it. A credential in a query string, in a body field, or in an app-specific
# header (`X-Api-Key`, `X-Auth`) is invisible to this rule and to the probe's stripping alike —
# the tier judges what it can attribute, and says so rather than guessing at a header vocabulary it
# does not own. An interpolated header *name* is skipped for the same reason: the checker would be
# guessing at what the name resolves to, and a false TF062 on a correct file is worse than a missed
# one on a file that has already opted out of being readable.
#
# **The step it reads is the nearest preceding `api` step**, which is exactly the step the assertion
# is about — the same one whose observed request the probe rebuilds. That is why the second test
# below is silent: the credential sits on an *earlier* request, and the request being judged carries
# none.
#
# **Its runtime twin snapshots rather than re-reads, and that distinction is load-bearing.** The
# interpreter carries the same guard, and it compares against what the owner's sessions contributed
# **as of the observed request**, not as of the assertion. `refreshSessions` mutates the session
# headers in place on a 401, so an assertion-time read would report an ordinary mid-test token
# refresh as a step naming its own credential. Both guards run before any probe is sent.
#
# **Not covered:** whether the hand-written credential is *valid*. Nothing here dials anything —
# this is a check-only fixture, and the defect is that the identity is unattributable, not that it
# is wrong.
#
# This project's `tflw.config` declares no `authorized target`, so TF060 fires here too — permission
# to scan and attributability of the identity are independent requirements. See
# security-without-authorized-target.tflw for that half.
#
# Run explicitly (dot-directory, invalid syntax — see bad-keyword.tflw's header for why):
#   npx tflw check tests/.checkonly/authz-step-names-own-credential.tflw

test "a bearer written onto the step the assertion judges" as shopper
  api GET /orders
    header "Authorization" is "Bearer not-a-session-token"
  expect response has no authorization violations

test "a cookie is the same defect in the other transport" as shopper
  api GET /orders
    header "Cookie" is "session=not-a-session-token"
  expect response has no authorization violations

test "the case of the header name is not a way out" as shopper
  api GET /orders
    header "authorization" is "Bearer not-a-session-token"
  expect response has no authorization violations

test "the neighbours the rule must not touch" as shopper
  # A credential on an *earlier* request. The step this assertion judges carries none, so the
  # owner is exactly what `as shopper` contributed and the comparison has two nameable sides.
  api POST /auth/login body { email: "a@example.com", password: "pw" }
    header "Authorization" is "Bearer bootstrap"

  api GET /orders
    header "Accept" is "application/json"
  expect response has no authorization violations
